      sources: "Sources: Learn, List definitions ($filter); Update accessReviewScheduleDefinition; tutorial (delete removes instances). Chapter 07, “The dry run, the diff and -Apply.”",
      say: `The dry run reads the tenant first, matches each series by name, and compares only what the file sets, ignoring defaults Graph adds on its own. Each series gets an action. New ones are created. Changed settings are updated in place, for future reviews only. But if the scope changes, Learn's update API won't take it, so the code marks it for replacement and leaves that to a person, because deleting a review deletes its decisions, and those are evidence. And the apply refuses to run at all while the plan has any finding.`,
      detail: `Get-PaArPlan reads the tenant with GET only, matches each series by name, and compares only the properties the JSON sets, ignoring the defaults Graph adds and a /v1.0 or /beta prefix on queries. Listing by name is deliberate: $filter isn't supported on principalResourceMembershipsScope. Each series gets an action. Create: not in the tenant. Update: settings, descriptions, fallback reviewers or user reviewers differ; the update is a PUT of the full object, and it applies only to future instances. Replace: the scope differs, or reviewers that are groups differ; the update API doesn't take a new scope, and takes new reviewers only when they are individual users; the code leaves these to a person. NoChange, Unmanaged (prefix, not in the JSON), Conflict (name used twice). Invoke-PaArApply without -Apply returns what it would send. With -Apply it refuses a plan that has any Finding, POSTs each Create, PUTs each Update with its ID, sends Azure series only with -IncludePreview, never deletes, and reads the series back. Replace needs a person on purpose: deleting a definition deletes its instances and decisions, which are evidence, so export them first.`,
