      say: `Who is this for? Developers build the pipeline. The identity governance officer owns the reviews themselves. And the ISSO reads the results. The permissions split the same way: the plan and evidence jobs only read, so they hold the read permission and Azure Reader. Only the apply identity can write, with User Access Administrator for Azure, the narrower of the two roles Learn names. Learn's pages disagree on which role can create a review, so the chapter follows the PIM page and applies as an app. A test keeps each task's permissions exactly what its calls need.`,
      detail: `Audience: developers who build the project's governance pipeline, the identity governance officer who owns the reviews (the plan's owner of A2.7 and A3.7), and the ISSO who reads their results. You should read PowerShell 7 and JSON. You don't need a tenant until Lab 2. Every call is in config/calls-ch07.json with its permission and Learn page, and a test keeps each task's permissions equal to the union of the calls it makes on the fixture. List definitions: AccessReview.Read.All, delegated Security Reader for reviews of Entra roles; plan, apply, evidence. List instances: AccessReview.Read.All; same; evidence. List decisions: AccessReview.Read.All; same; evidence. Create definition (POST): AccessReview.ReadWrite.All; Privileged Role Administrator for role reviews; apply. Update definition (PUT): AccessReview.ReadWrite.All; same; apply. Azure: list definitions, instances, decisions: Reader (*/read); plan, apply, evidence. Azure: create or update by ID (PUT, preview): User Access Administrator; apply, with -IncludePreview. So the plan and evidence identity holds AccessReview.Read.All and Reader; the apply identity holds AccessReview.ReadWrite.All and User Access Administrator on the subscriptions with an Azure series. Learn's PIM page asks for Owner or User Access Administrator for Azure reviews; User Access Administrator is the narrower. Learn's deployment plan names AccessReview.Read.All for a script that only queries reviews. Where Learn's pages differ: the role that creates a role review — the tutorial says at least Identity Governance Administrator; the API overview says User Administrator for create, update or delete; the PIM page says at least Privileged Role Administrator for reviews of Microsoft Entra roles. The program follows the PIM page, as Book 04, Chapter 08 does, and runs -Apply as an app with the application permission (research gaps, row 78). The role that reads a role review: the API overview lists Global Reader for reads; the list pages don't list Global Reader for reviews of Microsoft Entra roles. The code's delegated reader is Security Reader.`,
